            <note corresp="#d8-f2" n="2" xml:id="d8-f2-content">
                <p>
                    Nixon met with and Chairman Mao Tse-tung and Premier Zhou En-lai in the
                    People’s Republic of China, February 21–28, 1972.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d8-f3" n="3" xml:id="d8-f3-content">
                <p>
                    Nixon met with Soviet General Secretary
                        Leonid Brezhnev in Moscow, May 22–28,
                    1972.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d8-f4" n="4" xml:id="d8-f4-content">
                <p>Reference is to the “Interim Agreement Between the Union of Soviet Socialist
                    Republics and the United States of America on Certain Measures with Respect to
                    the Limitation of Strategic Offensive Arms,” better known as SALT I, signed
                    between President Nixon and Soviet
                    General Secretary Leonid Brezhnev in
                    Moscow on May 26, 1972. For the full text of the treaty, see <ref
                        target="frus1969-76v32#d317">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations,</hi> 1969–1976, Vol. XXXII, SALT
                        I, 1969–1972, Document 317</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d8-f5" n="5" xml:id="d8-f5-content">
                <p>Secretary of State Rogers signed the
                    “Agreement on Ending the War and Restoring Peace in Vietanm” in Paris on January
                    27, 1973. For the full text of the Agreement, see Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin,</hi> February 12, 1973, pp. 169–188.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d8-f6" n="6" xml:id="d8-f6-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d7-f2">footnote 2, Document 7</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
